WEBVTT

00:12:27.476 --> 00:12:30.596
generally they're found on the Somerset,

00:12:30.636 --> 00:12:32.556
the Dorset and the Yorkshire coastlines.

00:12:32.556 --> 00:12:37.236
And that's because you have the constant
erosion of the foreshore and the cliffs.

00:12:37.276 --> 00:12:38.956
You might be lucky to find, say,

00:12:38.996 --> 00:12:42.676
the isolated one or two vertebrae
on the beach if you go looking.

00:12:42.716 --> 00:12:45.156
But still finding a complete skeleton,

00:12:45.196 --> 00:12:48.796
and especially anything large
on the coastline is incredibly rare.

00:12:48.836 --> 00:12:52.516
This is why finding the Rutland
ichthyosaur in the center of the UK

00:12:52.516 --> 00:12:56.236
in an unusual exposure
is so extraordinarily rare.

00:12:58.196 --> 00:13:02.116
So what next for this
significant specimen?